# St. Claire — Places

The record of physical locations — buildings, public spaces, and sites — independent of whatever organization or household may occupy them. A Place can exist without any Organization (a park, the amphitheater, the cemetery) or without being residential.

## Place Record Template

- **Name** (or address-equivalent, if it doesn't have a proper name)
- **Zone** — one of the six zones in St_Claire_00_Concept.md, and why it fits there
- **Type** — commercial storefront, residential building, public space, industrial site, etc.
- **Physical description** — what it actually looks and feels like
- **History** — how long it's existed, what (if anything) occupied it before
- **Units** — a list, always present. A single-occupant Place has exactly one unit (no number needed). A multi-unit Place has one entry per unit/suite/apartment number. Each unit entry directly contains its own occupant(s):
  - An Organization (cross-referenced to St_Claire_05, tagged: primary tenant / servicing-contracted / secondary tenant), or
  - A named resident/Household (cross-referenced to St_Claire_03/St_Claire_04), or
  - "Vacant," or
  - For the Place as a whole rather than a unit — "public, unoccupied" (parks, civic space)

---

## Places

**Merchant's Blooms storefront**
- Zone: Downtown/Commercial Spine — fits the dense, foot-traffic-driven retail pattern established for that zone
- Type: commercial storefront
- Physical description: not yet determined
- History: not yet determined
- Units: one unit, occupied by Merchant's Blooms (primary tenant — cross-reference: St_Claire_05_Organizations.md)

**Apex Heights**
- Zone: Premium Riverfront
- Type: residential high-rise
- Physical description: glass and reclaimed-steel tower, panoramic riverfront views, smart home automation, marble finishes, 24-hour concierge
- History: the district's newest major construction — deliberately contemporary rather than historic, operating within the community's land-use/equity norms rather than as an unchecked gentrification threat
- Units: multi-unit, 90 residential units (expanded). Building manager: Desmond "Des" Miller (trans man, partnered) — manages the building with the understanding of someone who also lives and works in St. Claire

**Keystone Lofts and Market Hall**
- Zone: Working Riverfront (Old Town) — a converted former textile mill, consistent with the zone's uneven-revitalization industrial character
- Type: mixed-use — residential lofts above, public indoor market hall below
- Physical description: heavy timber framing, large multi-pane windows, an expansive central ground-floor atrium functioning as a public indoor market — the original volume preserved rather than gutted
- History: an industrial conversion, part of the district's uneven revitalization pattern. One of the Factory Quarter's two anchor institutions (alongside the Monarch Works — not yet given its own Place record)
- Units: multi-unit. Ground floor confirmed tenants: The River Mill Eatery (farm-to-table bistro), Kindred Brews (taproom & micro-cidery) — both cross-referenced in St_Claire_05_Organizations.md. Residential lofts above: 50 residential units.

**Greenleaf Stage Amphitheater**
- Zone: Downtown/Commercial Spine — the district's central gathering space (see St_Claire_00_Concept.md, Public Space & Civic Infrastructure)
- Type: public space
- Physical description: central amphitheater surrounded by terraced lawns and native plantings; hosts the Saturday farmers market
- History: the civic heart the rest of downtown radiates outward from
- Units: none — public, unoccupied

**St. Claire Parish School**
- Zone: Working Riverfront (Old Town), near the original parish site
- Type: educational institution
- Physical description: not yet determined
- History: carries the parish name per the naming pattern established in Origin & History — active school serving the whole community, not sized for the district's actual (smaller) child population, per Growth Pattern
- Units: one — occupied by St. Claire Public Academy & Childcare Center (cross-reference: St_Claire_05_Organizations.md), fully staffed

**St. Claire Parish Community Center & Gym**
- Zone: Working Riverfront (Old Town), the former parish's physical site
- Type: civic/nonprofit hub
- Physical description: not yet determined
- History: repurposed parish infrastructure — the likely physical home for the Safety Net & Mutual Aid institutions (see St_Claire_00_Concept.md)
- Units: one — occupant(s) not yet populated

**St. Claire Parish Sanctuary**
- Zone: Working Riverfront (Old Town), the former parish's physical site
- Type: civic space
- Physical description: not yet determined
- History: the actual former worship space, continuing the parish's pastoral function secularly — where the informal Mayor/council conducts mediation and guidance (see Informal Justice & Social Accountability)
- Units: one — public/civic use, associated with Ruth Halloway's Mayor function rather than a private tenant

**The Hearth Room** (senior center — relocated from the old draft's "Northern Hill Edge," which conflicted with the new Hills zone's premium-residential identity)
- Zone: Residential Infill — fits the zone's density and "everyone knows someone" daily-life character, and is a strong candidate to merge with the multigenerational daycare/mentoring center concept already established (see Aging & Mortality)
- Type: civic/elder care
- Physical description: sunroom windows full of afternoon light
- History: not yet determined
- Units: one — occupied by The Hearth Room (Organization; cross-reference: St_Claire_05_Organizations.md), led by Clara Higgins

**The Monarch Works**
- Zone: Working Riverfront (Old Town) — the Factory Quarter, alongside Keystone Lofts and Market Hall
- Type: mixed-use industrial conversion
- Physical description: sprawling former brick manufacturing plant; sawtooth glass roofs, polished concrete floors, cast-iron structural columns
- History: an industrial conversion, one of the Factory Quarter's two anchor institutions
- Units: multi-unit — commercial ground floor: The Crimson Kettle, Hearthside Bakery (cross-referenced in St_Claire_05_Organizations.md)

**Anchor Point**
- Zone: Residential Infill — land-trust affordable housing, consistent with the zone's daily-life/density character rather than premium status
- Type: residential — multi-family, income-controlled
- Physical description: modern, beautifully maintained multi-family buildings; accessible ground-floor units
- History: funded by the land trust; provides permanent housing security for residents who would otherwise be displaced
- Units: multi-unit, 70 residential units (expanded)

**The Hearthstone Co-Housing Collective**
- Zone: Residential Infill — co-housing/communal model, consistent with the zone's density and mutual-aid character
- Type: residential — co-housing
- Physical description: shared commercial kitchens, communal dinner tables, rooftop gardens
- History: built around a deliberate culture of mutual support
- Units: multi-unit, 40 private units around shared communal space (expanded)

