**Historic Brick Core** (shared multi-tenant Place — explicitly grouped in the compendium's Architectural Notes)
- Zone: Working Riverfront (Old Town) — the pre-zoning organic mixed-use fabric already established for this zone
- Type: commercial storefront row, historic brick
- Physical description: restored 19th-century buildings, warm red brick, arched windows, wide stoops, hand-lettered storefront signage — no uniform restoration aesthetic, organic accumulated maintenance rather than a preservation grant
- History: the district's oldest surviving residential and commercial layer
- Units: multi-tenant. Confirmed occupants: The Sunrise Counter, The Brass Ring, The Bindery, The Apothecary at 4th, Ink & Anchor, Thread Count & Tailoring, The Clipper & Shears Barbershop, Riverside Provisions, Petrosyan's Market, The Harbor House (all cross-referenced in St_Claire_05_Organizations.md). Upper floors: 60 residential units, the historic pattern of living above the shop.

**Greenleaf Core** (shared multi-tenant Place — the civic/commercial heart around Greenleaf Stage)
- Zone: Downtown/Commercial Spine
- Type: commercial storefront row, civic-adjacent
- Physical description: coffee shops, a printshop, and a community garden give the Core constant low-grade daily foot traffic
- History: the district's civic heart, organized around Greenleaf Stage
- Units: multi-tenant. Ground-floor commercial: L'Aura, Amour Noir, Root & Rind, Blush, The Velvet Palette, The Little Stage, Shift & Spin, The Nook, Second Chapter, The Delicatessen, The Inkwell & Ledger, Hearthside Real Estate & Brokerage, Ferris Family & Estate Law, Constance Ibarra – St. Claire Legal Aid Society, St. Claire Inn & Suites, St. Claire Convention Center, St. Claire Community Credit Union, Marion Castellano's St. Claire Beacon (all cross-referenced in St_Claire_05_Organizations.md). Upper floors: 50 residential units (expanded).

**Riverfront Fringe** (shared Place — the district's working eastern edge)
- Zone: Transition Zone — matches the compendium's own description of this area as "unglamorous," tradespeople-oriented, load-bearing rather than picturesque
- Type: mixed commercial/industrial
- Physical description: a veterinary hospital, an auto garage, a public-works sanitation yard running a biodiesel fleet; canvas jumpsuits before 6am
- History: not yet determined
- Units: multi-tenant. Little Paws (pet supply/dog park café) is a plausible fit here given the veterinary-adjacent character; other named tenants (a veterinary hospital, "Iron Horse Garage") not yet promoted as their own Organizations

**Incubator Belt** (shared Place — converted light-industrial corridor)
- Zone: Working Riverfront (Old Town) — interior-western edge, per the compendium
- Type: converted light-industrial
- Physical description: late-night freelancers, hardware prototypes, people building things they don't have names for yet
- History: not yet determined
- Units: multi-tenant. Confirmed: The Chrysalis Co-Op, The Nexus Incubator, The Maker's Crucible (each also has its own individual Place entry above — this entry represents the shared corridor they sit in). Also confirmed: Thread & Timber, The Open Page, The Daily Grind (all cross-referenced in St_Claire_05_Organizations.md).

**Residential Infill Commons** (shared multi-tenant Place — the everyday commercial strip within the residential zone)
- Zone: Residential Infill
- Type: commercial storefront row, neighborhood-scale
- Physical description: not yet determined
- History: not yet determined
- Units: multi-tenant. Confirmed occupants: Cornerstone Kitchen, Iron & Grace, Rooted Resilience, The Femme Care & Aesthetics Collective, Stride & Spoke, Sanctuary, Starlight Cinema (all cross-referenced in St_Claire_05_Organizations.md)

**Sage & Soil (garden site)**
- Zone: Working Riverfront (Old Town) — consistent with the Urban Agriculture pattern established for this zone
- Type: community garden, reclaimed land
- Physical description: turned earth and compost bins in the first morning light; greenhouse haze of humidity and green
- History: not yet determined — the composting system itself was designed fifteen years ago (see St_Claire_05_Organizations.md)
- Units: one — occupied by Sage & Soil (cross-reference: St_Claire_05_Organizations.md)

**The Fourth Street Plaza** (strip mall)
- Zone: Transition Zone — fits the zone's unglamorous, utilitarian character
- Type: strip mall
- Physical description: a single-story row of storefronts facing a shared parking lot, generic construction, functional rather than distinctive
- History: not yet determined
- Units: 6 units. Tenant mix: a nail salon, a laundromat, an insurance/tax office, a phone repair shop, a fast-casual chain outlet, one vacant unit. None of these are named Organizations — deliberately generic, background infrastructure rather than distinctive district character.

**Cedar Crossing Plaza** (strip mall)
- Zone: Residential Infill — the plain, chain-store counterpart to the Residential Infill Commons' characterful neighborhood businesses nearby
- Type: strip mall
- Physical description: a single-story row of storefronts facing a shared parking lot, generic construction, functional rather than distinctive
- History: not yet determined
- Units: 5 units. Tenant mix: a dollar-store-type shop, a check-cashing/phone-repair storefront, a fast-casual chain outlet, a dry cleaner, one vacant unit. None of these are named Organizations — deliberately generic.

**The Old Cordage Works**
- Zone: Working Riverfront (Old Town) — the working riverfront's least-revitalized stretch
- Type: abandoned industrial
- Physical description: a former rope-manufacturing plant, broken windows, collapsed sections of roof, decades of graffiti layered over decades of rust
- History: closed alongside much of the original riverfront industrial economy; never redeveloped, unlike Keystone Lofts and the Monarch Works nearby
- Units: none — vacant, unoccupied, structurally unsound in places
- Redevelopment potential: substantial. Large open floor plates similar to Keystone Lofts — a strong candidate for residential loft conversion (roughly 15-20 units) or a mixed live/work building. The next real "next Keystone Lofts" if St. Claire's revitalization ever reaches this stretch.

**Pier 9 Grain Terminal**
- Zone: Working Riverfront (Old Town) — directly on the water, part of the original barge-logistics economy
- Type: abandoned industrial
- Physical description: towering concrete grain silos, rusted conveyor gantries, a real landmark visible from much of the riverfront despite being empty
- History: closed with the decline of the district's original barge-shipping economy; too structurally complex/expensive to convert
- Units: none — vacant, unoccupied
- Redevelopment potential: limited but real. The silo structure itself resists conventional conversion (real-world grain-silo conversions exist but are expensive and architecturally unusual) — any future use here would likely be a single striking boutique project (a landmark restaurant, an arts venue, unique residential) rather than practical multi-unit housing or ordinary small business space.

**The Anchor Street Machine Shop**
- Zone: Working Riverfront (Old Town) — smaller-scale, tucked between occupied buildings
- Type: abandoned industrial
- Physical description: a modest single-story shop, boarded windows, faded hand-painted signage still legible
- History: not yet determined
- Units: none — vacant, unoccupied
- Redevelopment potential: modest and practical. Small enough for a single storefront business (1-2 units) — the kind of low-stakes, low-cost redevelopment that fits a business just starting out, consistent with the "butch mechanic starting out here" pattern already established for this zone.

